\section{Analysis: Entropy-Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:analysis}

The results in \S\ref{sec:experiments} reveal a striking asymmetry: importance-based eviction exhibits $12$--$75\times$ severity amplification in low-sparsity phases, while random eviction remains flat at ${\sim}1\times$ across all phases. Why is compound severity phase-modulated for one strategy but not the other? We propose \emph{entropy-discrimination collapse} as an explanatory mechanism: head pruning redistributes attention mass in a phase-dependent manner, degrading the discriminative power of importance scores precisely in phases where attention is already broadly distributed.

\subsection{Attention Redistribution Under Head Pruning}
\label{sec:attn-redistribution}

When a fraction $s$ of attention heads are pruned, the surviving heads must compensate for the lost information pathways. We characterize this compensation through the attention entropy of a surviving head $h$ at query position $i$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_i^{(h)} = -\sum_{j=1}^{T} a_{ij}^{(h)} \log a_{ij}^{(h)}
    \label{eq:attn-entropy}
\end{equation}
Higher $\mathcal{H}$ indicates more uniformly distributed attention. The effect of pruning on $\mathcal{H}$ depends critically on the reasoning phase.

In the \textbf{high-sparsity phase}, attention is already concentrated on a few key positions ($\mathcal{H}$ is low). Head redundancy is high---concentrated attention patterns are likely to overlap across heads---so pruning removes largely redundant capacity. The surviving heads continue to attend to the same narrow set of positions, and $\mathcal{H}$ changes minimally.

In the \textbf{low-sparsity phase}, attention distributes broadly across many positions ($\mathcal{H}$ is already elevated). Head redundancy is low: each head captures partially non-overlapping information. Pruning forces surviving heads to broaden their attention further to compensate for the lost coverage, pushing $\mathcal{H}$ above its already high baseline.

\placeholder{Empirical attention entropy: mean $\mathcal{H}$ per phase, pre- and post-pruning at $s \in \{0.15, 0.25\}$, showing differential entropy increase concentrated in the low-sparsity phase.}

\subsection{Discrimination Collapse in Importance Scoring}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse}

The KV importance score (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}) aggregates attention mass across all surviving heads. When pruning increases the attention entropy of these heads, the cumulative importance scores become more uniform: the gap between the most-attended and least-attended positions shrinks. We hypothesize that this uniformity degrades the \emph{discriminative power} of importance-based eviction---the ability to reliably distinguish positions critical for the model's computation from those that can be safely removed.

When the importance distribution has low entropy (a few positions dominate), thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction removes genuinely unimportant positions. When the distribution is near-uniform, the same thresholding becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform importance scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction.

\placeholder{Importance score concentration (Gini coefficient or max-to-median ratio) across phases, pre- and post-pruning, showing discrimination collapse predominantly in the low-sparsity phase.}

This mechanism directly accounts for the crossover phenomenon (\S\ref{sec:crossover}). In high-sparsity phases, pruning preserves attention concentration, importance scores remain discriminative, and importance-based eviction removes genuinely less critical positions---achieving lower compound severity than random eviction. In low-sparsity phases, pruning amplifies attention entropy, importance discrimination collapses, and importance-based eviction devolves into a biased pseudo-random process that can inadvertently remove critical positions---producing \emph{higher} compound severity than the explicitly random baseline. The severity inversion is thus not a failure of the importance metric in isolation, but an emergent consequence of the interaction between head pruning and importance scoring conditioned on the phase-dependent attention structure.

Random eviction is immune to this mechanism: its decisions are independent of attention patterns and invariant to the redistribution caused by head pruning. This explains the flat severity gradient ($G \approx 1$) observed under random eviction at all sparsity levels.

\subsection{Implications for Compound Sparsity}
\label{sec:analysis-implications}

The entropy-discrimination collapse mechanism suggests several broader conclusions.

